WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:05.964
This briefing from the Federal Modernization
Workbook asks one practical question:

3
00:00:05.964 --> 00:00:08.236
when there's no money for new products,

4
00:00:08.236 --> 00:00:12.412
can our own staff build the day-two
automation we need, and do it safely?

5
00:00:12.412 --> 00:00:17.120
By day two, I mean the everyday work
after a system goes live: the resets,

6
00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:21.250
the changes, the fixes, and the
proof that they were done right.

7
00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:23.509
I'll cover the problem
we're trying to fix,

8
00:00:23.509 --> 00:00:25.768
why the funded options are off the table,

9
00:00:25.768 --> 00:00:29.532
what we can build on the ServiceNow
instance we already own, and how

10
00:00:29.532 --> 00:00:34.050
artificial intelligence, or AI, can help
write the code without cutting corners.

11
00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:38.412
Then who does what, the evidence we
get out of it, and the honest limits.

12
00:00:38.412 --> 00:00:42.300
It ends with one small,
low-risk decision to approve.

13
00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:44.437
Let's start with the problem.

14
00:00:44.437 --> 00:00:47.938
A lot of day-two work still
runs on email and memory.

15
00:00:47.938 --> 00:00:51.044
Someone asks for a change,
someone else makes it,

16
00:00:51.044 --> 00:00:54.388
and the proof lives in an
inbox, if it lives anywhere.

17
00:00:54.388 --> 00:00:56.262
The work may well be done right.

18
00:00:56.262 --> 00:01:00.275
But when an auditor asks who approved
it, when it happened, and whether the

19
00:01:00.275 --> 00:01:04.287
fix actually held, there's nothing to
show except people's recollections.

20
00:01:04.287 --> 00:01:08.763
Even where there is a ticket, a closed
ticket only proves somebody did something.

21
00:01:08.763 --> 00:01:11.700
It doesn't prove the setting
stayed fixed the next day.

22
00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:14.901
An assessor wants four
things: an owner, a clock,

23
00:01:14.901 --> 00:01:18.187
an approval, and a result
someone else can check.

24
00:01:18.187 --> 00:01:19.213
That's the gap.

25
00:01:19.213 --> 00:01:22.040
We do the work, and
then we can't prove it.

26
00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:25.065
The usual answer is to buy our way out.

27
00:01:25.065 --> 00:01:30.090
ServiceNow sells compliance and
continuous-authorization applications that do much

28
00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:32.774
of this, and identity-governance
products such

29
00:01:32.774 --> 00:01:35.390
as SailPoint run access
reviews and a lot more.

30
00:01:35.390 --> 00:01:38.028
Microsoft has its own
governance features too.

31
00:01:38.028 --> 00:01:41.574
But ServiceNow's own documentation
lists those compliance

32
00:01:41.574 --> 00:01:45.689
applications as separate subscriptions,
on top of what we already pay.

33
00:01:45.689 --> 00:01:48.894
An identity-governance product
is another purchase again,

34
00:01:48.894 --> 00:01:51.640
and Microsoft's features
need their own licensing.

35
00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:54.890
If the answer on funding is
no, we have two choices.

36
00:01:54.890 --> 00:01:58.408
We can leave the design on paper
and keep doing things by email,

37
00:01:58.408 --> 00:02:02.340
or we can build the essential pieces
ourselves on what we already own.

38
00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:06.220
This briefing is about the
second choice, done carefully.

39
00:02:06.970 --> 00:02:08.807
So what can staff actually build?

40
00:02:08.807 --> 00:02:13.872
A small application inside the ServiceNow
instance we already license, using

41
00:02:13.872 --> 00:02:18.782
features that come with it, like its
tables, scripts, workflows and test tools.

42
00:02:18.782 --> 00:02:23.396
It records each finding, matches it to
the right asset in the configuration

43
00:02:23.396 --> 00:02:28.233
management database, and only closes work
when a fresh check shows the fix held.

44
00:02:28.233 --> 00:02:30.957
What it won't do is replace
a commercial product.

45
00:02:30.957 --> 00:02:34.758
No large-scale review campaigns,
no library of connectors,

46
00:02:34.758 --> 00:02:38.796
no machine-learning role
suggestions, no vendor support line.

47
00:02:38.796 --> 00:02:42.145
We should write that down up front
so nobody expects more later.

48
00:02:42.145 --> 00:02:43.845
And we don't start from scratch.

49
00:02:43.845 --> 00:02:48.709
An infrastructure team that already writes
its own ServiceNow scripts can lend us

50
00:02:48.709 --> 00:02:53.220
its conventions, its test instance and
its reviewers, and we can lend ours back.

51
00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:55.630
Neither team approves its own work.

52
00:02:56.380 --> 00:02:59.855
AI coding assistants can draft
these scripts in seconds.

53
00:02:59.855 --> 00:03:02.910
The catch is that they can
draft mistakes just as fast,

54
00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:04.993
and they sound confident either way.

55
00:03:04.993 --> 00:03:06.467
So the rules are simple.

56
00:03:06.467 --> 00:03:10.585
First, use only tools the agency
has approved under its AI policy,

57
00:03:10.585 --> 00:03:16.879
which the Office of Management and Budget's
memo M-25-21 asks every agency to have.

58
00:03:16.879 --> 00:03:19.754
Second, never put real data, passwords,

59
00:03:19.754 --> 00:03:24.018
or secrets into a prompt; we
use made-up examples instead.

60
00:03:24.018 --> 00:03:28.193
Third, a named person reads
every line before it's accepted.

61
00:03:28.193 --> 00:03:33.668
NIST, the National Institute of Standards
and Technology, takes the same position:

62
00:03:33.668 --> 00:03:37.693
AI-written code is reviewed
exactly like code a person wrote.

63
00:03:37.693 --> 00:03:41.620
And because assistants sometimes
invent functions that don't exist,

64
00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:45.617
every platform call gets checked
against the vendor's documentation.

65
00:03:45.617 --> 00:03:49.570
"The AI wrote it" is never
a reason to accept a line.

66
00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:51.820
Here's how the code moves.

67
00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:55.708
It starts in a developer sandbox
that holds only made-up data.

68
00:03:55.708 --> 00:03:59.101
ServiceNow gives developers
these sandboxes free,

69
00:03:59.101 --> 00:04:03.046
but they belong to the vendor, so
nothing real ever goes in one.

70
00:04:03.046 --> 00:04:05.123
Before code leaves the sandbox,

71
00:04:05.123 --> 00:04:09.758
a reviewer who didn't write it reads
every line, and that's the first gate.

72
00:04:09.758 --> 00:04:12.143
Then it moves to our own test instance,

73
00:04:12.143 --> 00:04:15.683
where automated tests run
and the results are attached.

74
00:04:15.683 --> 00:04:18.219
The second gate is the
change advisory board,

75
00:04:18.219 --> 00:04:21.646
which approves the move to
production like any other change.

76
00:04:21.646 --> 00:04:24.820
Once it's approved, someone
other than the author puts it in.

77
00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:29.708
If a test fails along the way, the fix
goes back to the sandbox and starts again.

78
00:04:29.708 --> 00:04:33.430
Nobody moves their own code,
and nothing skips a gate.

79
00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:36.455
None of this is invented for the occasion.

80
00:04:36.455 --> 00:04:39.385
It's the standard software
development life cycle

81
00:04:39.385 --> 00:04:42.455
that our existing security
controls already expect.

82
00:04:42.455 --> 00:04:45.455
Changes are reviewed and
approved before they're made.

83
00:04:45.455 --> 00:04:47.505
They're tested before they're finalized.

84
00:04:47.505 --> 00:04:52.042
The person who writes the code isn't the
person who approves it or deploys it.

85
00:04:52.042 --> 00:04:56.741
And NIST's Secure Software Development
Framework gives us the review and testing

86
00:04:56.741 --> 00:05:01.231
practices to follow, along with a companion
profile written for generative AI.

87
00:05:01.231 --> 00:05:05.374
So walking this path means meeting
requirements we already have,

88
00:05:05.374 --> 00:05:06.755
not taking on new ones.

89
00:05:06.755 --> 00:05:10.409
It also means that if an assessor
asks how we control our own code,

90
00:05:10.409 --> 00:05:13.670
we can point to the same
controls they already know.

91
00:05:14.420 --> 00:05:15.545
So who does what?

92
00:05:15.545 --> 00:05:18.308
Developers on our own
staff write the code.

93
00:05:18.308 --> 00:05:21.783
A different, qualified
reviewer approves each change.

94
00:05:21.783 --> 00:05:25.144
The change advisory board
approves the move to production.

95
00:05:25.144 --> 00:05:27.898
The security officer
sets the coding rules,

96
00:05:27.898 --> 00:05:32.257
and the agency's Chief AI Officer
decides which AI tools are approved.

97
00:05:32.257 --> 00:05:36.619
The point is that no one person
writes, approves, and deploys.

98
00:05:36.619 --> 00:05:39.658
And we train the people we
already have rather than hiring.

99
00:05:39.658 --> 00:05:44.272
They start in a free developer sandbox,
learn our rules before they touch real work,

100
00:05:44.272 --> 00:05:48.683
and pair up with experienced platform
developers for their first few changes.

101
00:05:48.683 --> 00:05:52.157
After that, they can qualify
as reviewers themselves.

102
00:05:52.157 --> 00:05:55.793
Every piece should have at least
two people who can maintain it,

103
00:05:55.793 --> 00:05:58.880
so the application never
depends on one person.

104
00:05:59.630 --> 00:06:01.742
This is the part I most want you to see.

105
00:06:01.742 --> 00:06:05.519
Every gate leaves a record
behind: the review and who did it,

106
00:06:05.519 --> 00:06:09.768
the test results, the change
approval, and who deployed the change.

107
00:06:09.768 --> 00:06:14.424
Together, those records are the audit
evidence, and we get them as a by-product

108
00:06:14.424 --> 00:06:19.006
of doing the work properly, not by
assembling a binder before an assessment.

109
00:06:19.006 --> 00:06:22.859
When an assessor asks how we know
our own code does what we claim,

110
00:06:22.859 --> 00:06:26.417
the answer is a report we can
pull, not a story we have to tell.

111
00:06:26.417 --> 00:06:31.278
And the application's own output, its
control tests and its closed findings,

112
00:06:31.278 --> 00:06:34.492
joins the same evidence
trail as everything else.

113
00:06:34.492 --> 00:06:36.807
The assessor shouldn't be able to tell,

114
00:06:36.807 --> 00:06:40.300
or need to care, whether the
code was bought or built.

115
00:06:41.050 --> 00:06:42.538
Now the honest limits.

116
00:06:42.538 --> 00:06:43.651
This isn't free.

117
00:06:43.651 --> 00:06:46.766
It moves the cost from a
license line to staff time,

118
00:06:46.766 --> 00:06:50.413
including retesting every time
the platform is upgraded.

119
00:06:50.413 --> 00:06:53.776
It doesn't replace a full
identity-governance product,

120
00:06:53.776 --> 00:06:57.812
and if our needs grow, this buys
time rather than removing the need.

121
00:06:57.812 --> 00:07:02.308
Review is only as good as the
reviewer, and AI can produce confident,

122
00:07:02.308 --> 00:07:07.212
wrong code, so small changes and a
reviewer's right to say no really matter.

123
00:07:07.212 --> 00:07:11.183
The federal AI policy and the NIST
guidance are both still moving,

124
00:07:11.183 --> 00:07:12.874
so we'll need to recheck them.

125
00:07:12.874 --> 00:07:16.051
And the developer sandbox
belongs to the vendor,

126
00:07:16.051 --> 00:07:19.990
outside our boundary, so it
only ever holds made-up data.

127
00:07:20.740 --> 00:07:22.015
So here's the ask.

128
00:07:22.015 --> 00:07:24.840
Approve a small pilot in
a developer instance.

129
00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:26.190
Made-up data only.

130
00:07:26.190 --> 00:07:29.453
One or two staff, trained
on the rules first.

131
00:07:29.453 --> 00:07:33.665
An approved AI tool, and a named
reviewer who isn't the author.

132
00:07:33.665 --> 00:07:36.865
And one component: the check
that refuses to close a

133
00:07:36.865 --> 00:07:40.065
piece of work until a fresh
read proves the fix held.

134
00:07:40.065 --> 00:07:43.490
The code goes into an agency
repository from day one.

135
00:07:43.490 --> 00:07:47.459
At the end of the pilot, we come back
with the code, the test results,

136
00:07:47.459 --> 00:07:51.128
the review records, and an honest
list of what failed review.

137
00:07:51.128 --> 00:07:55.478
Then you decide whether it moves to our
test instance, through a normal change.

138
00:07:55.478 --> 00:07:58.497
It's small, it's reversible,
it needs no new money,

139
00:07:58.497 --> 00:08:02.750
and it shows you the evidence
before anyone commits to more.

140
00:08:03.500 --> 00:08:07.541
Everything in this briefing comes
from Book 15, Chapter 13 of the

141
00:08:07.541 --> 00:08:11.963
Federal Modernization Workbook, and
every source is footnoted there.

142
00:08:11.963 --> 00:08:15.507
Chapter 11 describes the
compliance application itself,

143
00:08:15.507 --> 00:08:17.574
Chapter 10 covers who runs day two,

144
00:08:17.574 --> 00:08:22.374
and the workbook's ServiceNow example is
a tested starting point for the pilot.

145
00:08:22.374 --> 00:08:23.730
Thank you for your time.
